  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 307 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nFriday through Sunday Night...\n\nUpper trough axis will push off to the east on Friday while a \nsecondary weak frontal boundary will drift southward through the \nregion.  With a lack of moisture, not expecting any precipitation \nwith this feature.  Highs will be in the 83-88 degree range with \novernight lows of 60-65.  For Saturday and Sunday, High pressure \nwill build into the region while an upper level ridge axis will \nbuild into the area from the west.  Mostly sunny days and mostly \nclear nights are expected through the period.  Highs on Saturday of \n84-89 are expected with overnight lows in the low-mid 60s. \nTemperatures will warm a bit more for Sunday.  The NBM blend here is \ntoo aggressive with highs in the lower 90s.  We expect temps to be \nin the 85-90 range.  Lows Sunday night will be in the upper 60s to \nnear 60.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nMoving into next week, there remains a strong signal in the guidance \nfor continued dry conditions with increasing temperatures.  Aloft, \nthe upper level ridge will dampen with time as a mid-level trough \naxis works into the Pacific Northwest.  Surface high pressure will \nbe off to our east and that will place the region in a favorable \nwarm advection pattern.  ECMWF EFI remains strongly suggestive of a \nwarmer than normal pattern compared to climatology.  The NBM \nguidance continues to show its high bias here and is too aggressive \nwith warming temps into the middle 90s.  We're likely to see temps \nMonday and Tuesday warm into the upper 80s to the lower 90s with \nmainly lower 90s for highs by Wednesday.  Dewpoints will remain in \nthe 60s through the period which will keep heat indices below 100 \nthrough the period. \n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nLooking beyond Wednesday, the long term ensembles and AI Ensemble \nguidance suggest the upper level pattern will undergo some changes. \nThe upper level ridge that is expected to bring dry/warm weather for \nmuch of the week is expected to break down as a series of mid-level \nwaves push across the CONUS.  While the details are not overly \nclear, it does look like that we'll have some passing upper troughs \nthat will drive some cold frontal boundaries through the region. \nWhile much of next week will feature above normal temperatures and \nbelow normal precipitation, we expect temps to come down a bit to \nnear normal with precipitation going up to normal or slightly above \nnormal.",
